mk("gIVUpqVRf-w","How to Structure Melodic House Tracks Like a Pro",
 "loop-is-the-climax-give-away-arrangement-reference-section-notes-every-8-bars-high-passed-kick-intro-thin-filtered-bass-root-pedal-before-progression-kick-gaps-before-drop-mirrored-outro",
 "Loop-to-track arrangement where the finished loop is the climax and everything before it 'gives away' that loop gradually: whole elements, the power of elements (high-passed kick, thin high-and-low-passed bass), and the chord progression itself (root-note pedal first), with a change at every 8-bar boundary and an outro that mirrors the intro",
 "Arrange a finished loop into a full track. First map a reference track: listen through and drop markers for what enters or leaves at each section (every 8 bars something changes). Treat your loop as the climax and plan how to reach it by giving things away over time. Drums first: copy the full drum loop to the start, high-pass the kick at about 120 Hz for the first 16 bars, start with fewer parts (no shaker/hats), bring shaker in, save hats and snare for later, and remove the kick's last bar (or last two bars) before each full-kick entry or drop. Atmosphere runs throughout. Bass: start as a single root-note pedal (monotonic) instead of the progression, with an octave jump at the end of each 8 bars; give away its power with labelled high-pass and low-pass filters automated from thin and dark to full. Melodic parts: bring in stripped-back fragments (first bar only, fewer notes), filter elements in instead of hard entries, reveal the full chord progression and full lead later; hold back some parts for the second drop. Outro: do the opposite of the intro (high-pass the kick again for 8 bars, filter the bass out and darker, return to the root pedal and a resolving last note).",
 ["A DAW with arrangement view, markers and filter automation","A finished 8-16 bar loop (drums, bass, chords, lead, atmosphere)","Optional: a reference track in a similar style"],
 [("loop","project","The finished loop, treated as the climax",True),("reference_track","audio","A similar-vibe track to map sections from",False)],
 [("arrangement","daw-arrangement","Intro → build → drop → breakdown → drop 2 → outro with staged give-aways"),("section_map","text","Marker list of what enters/leaves every 8 bars")],
 ["Copy only the structure from the reference, never its audio or melodies.",
  "Label filters (HP / LP) on each part before automating; there will be many lanes.",
  "Ambient/cinematic adaptation: keep the same give-away logic but swap the kick for the main pulse layer; Matt prefers drums entering early and steadily, so give away kick power (high-pass) rather than delaying the kick entirely.",
  "Transitions (reverse crashes, risers) and micro automation are a separate pass; this skill covers macro structure only.",A],
 [("Import a reference track, listen through and drop a marker each time something enters, leaves or changes (expect a change every 8 bars). Write the macro structure, e.g. intro, bass in, build, drop, breakdown, drop 2, outro.","DAW markers","A written section map."),
  ("List what you can give away: whole elements; element power (kick without lows, bass without sub, thinner versions); the chord progression; stripped-back versions of parts.","Notes","A give-away plan with the loop as the climax."),
  ("Drums first: copy the full drum loop to the start; high-pass the kick at about 120 Hz for the first 16 bars; remove shaker, hats and snare at first and stage them in later (shaker early, hats and snare saved for later).","DAW + filter automation","A thin but steady pulse from bar 1."),
  ("Delete the kick's last bar (or last 2 bars) before each full-kick entry or drop.","Arrangement","A gap that adds contrast to the entry."),
  ("Run the atmosphere/texture throughout to set the tone.","Arrangement","Continuous glue under all sections."),
  ("Bass: write a single root-note pedal for the early sections with an octave jump at the end of each 8 bars. Add labelled HP and LP filters and automate from thin and dark to full and bright; switch to the full chord progression only at the main section.","Bass track + filters","The harmony and the low-end power are revealed later."),
  ("Melodic parts: enter with fragments (first bar only, fewer notes), filter them in rather than hard-cutting, then reveal the full progression, full lead and extra layers (piano chords, high pluck) only at later peaks; hold some back for drop 2.","Arrangement + auto filter","Each 8-bar section adds or reveals something; the climax isn't spent early."),
  ("Before a drop, filter percussion/kick from thin and clicky to full, leave a short moment of near-silence, then hit the drop with everything un-high-passed.","Automation","A clear inflection into the drop."),
  ("Outro: mirror the intro: high-pass the kick for 8 bars, filter the bass out and darker, return to the root pedal and end on a resolving note.","Automation","The track closes in reverse order of the intro.")],
 ["Every 8-bar boundary has at least one entry, exit or filter change.","Kick is high-passed (about 120 Hz) in the intro and again in the outro; a 1-2 bar kick gap precedes each drop.","The full chord progression is not heard until the main section; earlier bass plays the root pedal."],
 ["arrangement","song-structure","loop-to-song","subtractive-arrangement","filter-automation","reference-track","breakdown","outro"],A)

mk("xY7vRHiSJSM","turn your loop into a story: an arrangement masterclass",
 "four-levels-loop-to-story-arrangement-subtractive-lego-blocks-energy-contour-turnaround-and-marker-transitions-performed-protagonist-crescendo-element-additive-cinematic-start-from-silence",
 "Four levels of loop-to-story arrangement: (1) subtractive Lego-block arrangement from a duplicated loop shaped by an energy contour, (2) turnaround-and-marker transitions at section edges, (3) a performed protagonist element that crescendos and decrescendos across sections, (4) an additive cinematic pass that starts from silence and adds only motivated elements",
 "Turn a 16-bar loop into a musical story in four escalating levels. Level 1 (subtractive): duplicate the loop across the whole target length (4-5 minutes), then cut away in 8- or 16-bar blocks following an energy contour (low start, build to the middle, kick-out dips, peak, outro); add a little silence at the start and room at the end for reverb tails. Level 2 (transitions): at each section edge add a turnaround (signals the end: remove low energy, reverse reverb swell or noise riser) and a marker on the downbeat (a splash: white noise, crash, one percussion hit into big reverb, stutter or false drop), built from the track's own timbres resampled, reversed or processed; transitions can be explicit or hypnotic. Level 3 (performance): record a performed element (e.g. filter, level or intensity moves on one sound) that starts mellow, grows to dominant and subsides, spanning section boundaries as the 'protagonist'; split and move the audio if needed. Level 4 (additive): start from silence and add elements only when the music is motivated (when you would get bored), like a film composer, letting well-designed elements play for a long time.",
 ["A DAW with duplicate-time, insert-time and split commands","A finished loop (16 bars in the video)","A controller or automation for performing one element (optional)"],
 [("loop","project","A dense 8-16 bar loop",True),("target_length_min","number","Track length, e.g. 4-5 minutes",True),("approach","enum","subtractive | additive (cinematic)",False)],
 [("arrangement","daw-arrangement","Section-based arrangement with transitions and a performed protagonist element"),("energy_contour","text","Sketch of the intended energy curve over time")],
 ["DAW shortcuts in the video are product-specific; use the equivalent duplicate-time / insert-silence / split commands in Ableton or any DAW.",
  "For Matt's ambient/cinematic system the additive level 4 is the target; levels 1-3 are scaffolding. Keep drums entering early and steady even in the additive pass if the piece has a beat.",
  "Performed elements should move musically and gradually so split/moved takes still join cleanly.",A],
 [("Decide the target length; select the loop and duplicate it end to end until the timeline exceeds that length; trim any extra.","DAW duplicate time","A 'block of clay' filling the whole song length."),
  ("Sketch an energy contour: low start, additions toward the middle, dips where the kick goes away, a maximum, then an outro. Optionally align a reference track to the grid and copy its arrangement (not its sounds).","Notes / reference track","An energy plan to subtract against."),
  ("Subtract in 8- or 16-bar blocks so each section boundary adds some elements and removes others. Insert a little silence at the start and leave space at the end for reverb tails.","Arrangement view","A working Lego-block arrangement."),
  ("At each section edge, add a turnaround in the last bar(s): remove low-end energy and add a reverse reverb swell or noise riser built from the track's own sounds.","Automation, resampling, reverse","The listener senses a new section coming."),
  ("On the new section's downbeat, add a marker: white noise burst, crash, one percussion hit into a big reverb, a stutter, or a false drop (withhold the first hit).","Arrangement","A clear splash of energy at the boundary."),
  ("Revisit breaks and drops: e.g. a very sparse moment, then build, build, build, then a short silence before the drop.","Arrangement + automation","Climaxes feel earned."),
  ("Pick one protagonist element and perform it across the whole track (record filter/level/intensity moves) so it starts mellow, grows dominant and subsides, crossing section boundaries; split and rearrange the performance if needed.","Controller / automation recording","A storyline that glues sections together; energy feels gradual and linear rather than A/B blocky."),
  ("Additive pass: from silence, peel everything back to almost nothing and add elements one at a time only when the music needs them (when you notice boredom), giving each element time and space.","Arrangement","A sparse, cinematic arrangement where every entry is motivated.")],
 ["Each section boundary has a turnaround before it and a marker on it (or is deliberately seamless).","One element's level or intensity curve rises then falls across at least three sections.","The track starts from silence or near-silence and ends with space for tails."],
 ["arrangement","song-structure","loop-to-song","transitions","energy-contour","crescendo","additive-arrangement","cinematic"],A)
